    <time:hasBeginning rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#August_7_1945"/>
    <time:hasEnd rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#September_2_1945"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://www.michaeldebellis.com/supply_chain_model/plannedSenderRole">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">planned sender role</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The contextual sender role for this planned message.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://www.michaeldebellis.com/supply_chain_model/MessageVariable"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://www.michaeldebellis.com/supply_chain_model/CollaborationRole"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="N4355f55f1e8c45acbe583a21ea0c13a4">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrl#AtomList"/>
    <rdf:first rdf:nodeID="Ne11d6d83c88f430692143ea0fdcad5e7"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="Nd326dc3bb8af4640b18197602ba8e087"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#Duration">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label>enduración de tiempo</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Time duration</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Duration of a temporal extent expressed as a number scaled by a temporal unit</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="es">Duración de una extensión temporal expresada como un número escalado por una unidad temporal.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="N15379b5589184bc1ab60a9166a72fca0"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="Ne5a2b8ee105f4cd99a3e18e33d524660"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#TemporalDuration"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Duration of a temporal extent expressed as a number scaled by a temporal unit</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="es">Duración de una extensión temporal expresada como un número escalado por una unidad temporal.</skos:definition>
    <skos:note xml:lang="en">Alternative to time:DurationDescription to support description of a temporal duration other than using a calendar/clock system.</skos:note>
    <skos:note xml:lang="es">Alternativa a 'descripción de tiempo' para proporcionar descripción soporte a una duración temporal diferente a utilizar un sistema de calendario/reloj.</skos:note>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#intervalDuring">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">interval during</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="es">intervalo durante</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">If a proper interval T1 is intervalDuring another proper interval T2, then the beginning of T1 is after the beginning of T2, and the end of T1 is before the end of T2.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="es">Si un intervalo propio T1 está durante otro intervalo propio T2, entonces del principio de T1 está después del principio de T2, y el final de T1 está antes que el final de T2.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#ProperInterval"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#ProperInterval"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#intervalIn"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">If a proper interval T1 is intervalDuring another proper interval T2, then the beginning of T1 is after the beginning of T2, and the end of T1 is before the end of T2.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="es">Si un intervalo propio T1 está durante otro intervalo propio T2, entonces del principio de T1 está después del principio de T2, y el final de T1 está antes que el final de T2.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://www.michaeldebellis.com/supply_chain_figure8">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Process modeling examples: tendering and Method Omega</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-10-22</dcterms:created>
    <dcterms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8824-9577"/>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">Self-contained experimental ontology containing the Figure 8 tendering Plan and its synthetic execution, plus the abstract Method Omega development methodology excerpt. Existing tendering assertions, vocabulary IRIs, SULO alignments and ten SWRL rules are retained. Method Omega adds nested P-Plan definitions, finish-to-start transitions, expected deliverable slots and an approval/rework choice. No Method Omega executions or estimate values are included.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:modified rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-06</dcterms:modified>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://doi.org/10.1080/00207543.2023.2242508"/>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://www.michaeldebellis.com/supply_chain_figure8/sourceSchemaMetadata"/>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://www.michaeldebellis.com/supply_chain_model"/>
    <dcterms:title xml:lang="en">Process modeling examples: collaborative tendering and Method Omega development</dcterms:title>
    <owl:versionInfo>figure8-and-method-omega-v1-2026-11-06</owl:versionInfo>
    <skos:changeNote xml:lang="en">2026-10-22 — Changed by ChatGPT. Implemented Figure 8 as a separate planned collaboration on the updated supply-chain schema. Added placeholder organizations, contextual role instances, seven task definitions and planned assignments, five planned events, ten control-flow transitions, ten resource/message variables, four message exchanges, and one explicit exclusive choice with approval and rejection conditions. Preserved the figure's seven-task structure by documenting t11 as a send-and-await request/reply task. Retained separate initial-draft, contribution, revised tender and submitted-tender variables, and the single-producer P-Plan semantics. The submitted-tender slot is an explicit completion artifact chosen for this model. Preserved all ten existing SWRL rules. No runtime execution, branch-selection result, synthetic test data or inferred assertions are included.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote xml:lang="en">2026-10-23 - Changed by ChatGPT. Corrected the inherited dcterms:created metadata value from 2025-28-11 to 2025-11-28 (interpreted as 28 November 2025) to address the reported AllegroGraph import error. Workflow definitions and rules are unchanged.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote xml:lang="en">2026-11-4 Changed by M. DeBellis. Several P-Plan properties had labels in CamelCase. I added skos:prefLabel values for them using my standard of all lower case for property labels. E.g., pplan:isStepOfPlan had label "isStepOfPlan". I added a skos pref label: "is step of plan" and made it a lang string datatype.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote xml:lang="en">2026-11-05 (ChatGPT): Restored ten complete SWRL rules and variable declarations from supply_chain_model(1).ttl, removed damaged SWRL structures and structural annotation-property declarations, and added a synthetic approved execution with six StepExecutions, one enclosing run Activity, seven OWL-Time intervals, ten realized resource variables and qualified PROV associations/usages. Preserved prior plan assertions and inverse-property edits. No inferred assertions or SHACL constraints added.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote xml:lang="en">2026-11-05 (ChatGPT): Completed the reused PROV-O subset for qualified usage by copying the standard declarations of Usage, EntityInfluence, qualifiedUsage, entity, qualifiedInfluence and influencer, including their original domain/range and hierarchy axioms. This prevents qualifiedUsage and entity being interpreted as annotation properties by OWLAPI. Existing data, rules and vocabulary IRIs are unchanged.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:changeNote xml:lang="en">2026-11-06 (ChatGPT): Added the Method Omega excerpt in the https://www.in2use.com/method_omega/ namespace, preserving existing domain data and all ten SWRL rules. Copied four missing standard P-Plan terms from the 2014 release. Added has_step and precedes display inverses, Finish_To_Start_Transition, exposes_output_variable, and expected_duration in https://www.in2use.com/process_model/. Kept separate MultiSteps and body Plans, single producers for Variables, and conditional rework without a precedence cycle. No Method Omega Activities, duration values, SHACL constraints or materialized inferences added. Updated ontology-level descriptive metadata; retained the ontology IRI.</skos:changeNote>
    <skos:note xml:lang="en">Control-flow transitions, message variables and decision conditions are explicit assertions. The existing SWRL rules infer candidate resource dependencies and shared inputs; they neither select a branch nor execute the plan. All declared steps and resource variables are explicitly distinct. Each produced variable has exactly one asserted producer. The call-for-tender variable is supplied externally.</skos:note>
    <skos:note xml:lang="en">Figure 8 returns the partner vote to t11SendTenderForVotingTask. This model interprets that task as sending the voting request and awaiting the reply. Consequently t11 and t12 have resource dependencies in both directions; these describe a request/reply exchange, not a control-flow loop or a reason to assert each task precedes the other. An executable refinement may split sending and receiving into separate steps. The replacement branch ends where the figure ends; no retry loop is added.</skos:note>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Figure 8 plan content follows the cited paper and its accompanying text; the dated tender run is explicitly synthetic. Method Omega follows Michael DeBellis's supplied diagram and conventions, with modeling choices explained on its individuals. Existing Time examples remain. These are review examples, not a workflow engine or records of real projects.</skos:scopeNote>
  </rdf:Description>
